From: Georg Seema <georgseema@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss: rmnet: leave GSO receive packets to the network stack

The raw-IP receive handler can receive GRO aggregates from kernel rmnet.
Linearizing these buffers and redirecting them to the NSS receive node
is not equivalent to segmenting them into the original packets. On an
RM551 using mhi_net and rmnet, IPv6 QUIC traffic reaches the client as
fragmented UDP datagrams with multiples of the original payload length.
Disabling GRO on the rmnet interface restores video playback.

Pass GSO buffers to the network stack before modifying them. Linux
retains the segmentation metadata and performs segmentation for the
selected egress device. Ordinary receive packets still use NSS. Keep
GRO enabled and avoid relying on firmware receive-side segmentation
support inferred from the generic transmit segmentation flag.

Signed-off-by: Georg Seema <georgseema@gmail.com>
---
 nss_rmnet_rx.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/nss_rmnet_rx.c b/nss_rmnet_rx.c
--- a/nss_rmnet_rx.c
+++ b/nss_rmnet_rx.c
@@ -944,6 +944,14 @@ static rx_handler_result_t nss_rmnet_rx_bind_handler(struct sk_buff **pskb)
 	struct ethhdr *eth;
 	__be16 proto, orig_proto;
 
+	/*
+	 * GRO aggregates require segmentation, not IP fragmentation. Leave
+	 * them intact for the stack: this receive node must not consume GSO
+	 * packets as ordinary frames, even after skb_linearize().
+	 */
+	if (skb_is_gso(skb))
+		return RX_HANDLER_PASS;
+
 	if (__this_cpu_read(nss_rmnet_rx_reinject)) {
 		return RX_HANDLER_PASS;
 	}
